Q: It&#39;s possible to draw MapPolygon with a hole on Map in QML? A: <p>The following QML code should work too, without the requirement for C++. Tested on Qt 6.5 and 6.9.</p>
<pre><code>import QtQuick 2.12
import QtQuick.Window 2.12
import QtLocation 5.13
import QtPositioning 5.13

Window {
    visible: true
    width: 640
    height: 480
    Map {
        anchors.fill: parent
        center: QtPositioning.coordinate(52, 22)
        plugin: Plugin {
            name: &quot;osm&quot;
        }
        zoomLevel: 4
        MapPolygon {
            color: &quot;red&quot;
            border.color: &quot;green&quot;
            border.width: 2
            smooth: true
            opacity: 0.25

            // Construct a polygon from a perimeter contour
            // and a list of inner hole contours.
            geoShape: QtPositioning.polygon(
                [
                    QtPositioning.coordinate(51.11, 17.13),
                    QtPositioning.coordinate(50.42, 30.54),
                    QtPositioning.coordinate(58.36, 26.70)
                ],
                [
                    [
                       QtPositioning.coordinate(54.36, 23.46),
                       QtPositioning.coordinate(51.91, 20.52),
                       QtPositioning.coordinate(51.50, 28.25),
                       QtPositioning.coordinate(54.36, 26.80)
                   ]
                ]
            )
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>

Q: How to intercept browser back button / trackpad swipe in React and redirect to a specific route instead of history back A: <p>you should not intercept popstate directly when using React Router. It bypasses the router’s own history handling and leads to exactly the inconsistencies you’re seeing.</p>
<p>React Router already listens to <code>popstate</code> internally. The correct approach is to model the behavior through routing state and navigation rules, not low-level browser events.</p>
<p>If you want Back to always go to a specific route, Use <strong>explicit navigation control</strong> instead of the browser back button logic.</p>
<p>When navigating <em>to</em> the page:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>navigate(&quot;/profile/edit&quot;, {
  state: { backTo: &quot;/settings&quot; }
});
</code></pre>
<p>Then inside the page:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>import { useLocation, useNavigate } from &quot;react-router-dom&quot;;

function EditProfile() {
  const navigate = useNavigate();
  const location = useLocation();

  useEffect(() =&gt; {
    return () =&gt; {
      if (location.state?.backTo) {
        navigate(location.state.backTo, { replace: true });
      }
    };
  }, [location, navigate]);

  return &lt;div&gt;Edit Profile&lt;/div&gt;;
}
</code></pre>
<p>This keeps routing logic declarative and predictable. Popstate is mostly never used for navigation routing. Hope this helps :)</p>

Q: IEEE754 floating point to struct and vice versa A: <p>If interested in portability, 1) do not assume the size of <code>unsigned</code> is the same as <code>float</code>.  Code is better off using <code>uint32_t</code> and failing the compile if sizes differ or <code>uint32_t</code> not available.  2) Keep in mind that the endian of an integer and a FP may differ.  Somewhat rare, but possible.</p>
<p>I'd recommend to <strong>not</strong> use bit fields, use union of <code>uint32_t</code>, <code>int32_t</code> and <code>float</code> with a few <code>static_assert</code>s and <code>assert()</code>s to validate size match, endian match, # of significand bits, expo range, sub-normal support, etc.</p>
<p>Extract FP bits as needed with shifts and masks.</p>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>// Illustrative code
#include &lt;assert.h&gt;
#include &lt;float.h&gt;
#include &lt;limits.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdbool.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdint.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

typedef union {
  float f;
  uint32_t u;
  int32_t i;
} ieee754_bin32_t;

int32_t JK_float_to_integer(float f) {
  ieee754_bin32_t x = {.f = f};
  return x.i;
}

float JK_integer_to_float(int32_t i) {
  ieee754_bin32_t x = {.i = i};
  return x.f;
}

bool JK_integer_to_sign(int32_t i) {
  return i &lt; 0;
}

uint32_t JK_integer_to_encoded_fraction(int32_t i) {
  return (uint32_t) i &amp; 0x7FFFFFu;
}

int JK_integer_to_encoded_exponent(int32_t i) {
  return ((uint32_t) i &gt;&gt; 23) &amp; 0xFF;
}

float JK_encoded_integer_to_float(bool sign, int expo, int32_t frac) {
  ieee754_bin32_t x = {.u = (sign ? 0x80000000u : 0u)
      | (((uint32_t) (expo &amp; 0xFF)) &lt;&lt; 23) | ((uint32_t) frac &amp; 0x7FFFFF)};
  return x.f;
}

// Following static_assert()s and assert()s are not absolute proof of
// ieee754 bin32 encoding, yet it will be very difficult to pass the tests
// and not have used the hope for encoding.

static_assert(sizeof(float)== sizeof(uint32_t), &quot;Unusual type sizes&quot;);

static_assert((FLT_RADIX == 2) &amp;&amp; (FLT_MANT_DIG == 24) &amp;&amp; (FLT_DIG == 6) &amp;&amp;
    (FLT_MIN_EXP == -125) &amp;&amp; (FLT_MIN_10_EXP == -37) &amp;&amp; (FLT_MAX_EXP == 128) &amp;&amp;
    (FLT_MAX_10_EXP == 38) &amp;&amp; (FLT_DIG == 6),
    &quot;float with unusual characteristics&quot;);

//////////////

int main() {
  assert(
      (FLT_EPSILON == 0x1p-23f) &amp;&amp; (FLT_MIN == 0x1p-126f) &amp;&amp; (FLT_TRUE_MIN == 0x1p-149f) &amp;&amp; (FLT_MAX == 0x1.fffffep+127f));
  assert(JK_integer_to_float(0x12345678 &gt;&gt; 1) == 0x1.345678p-109f);
}
</code></pre>
